  "Question": "For which real values of \\(m\\) are the simultaneous equations \\[ \\begin{aligned} y&=mx+3,\\\\ y&=(2m-1)x+4 \\end{aligned} \\] satisfied by at least one pair of real numbers \\((x,y)?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "all \\(m\\)",
    "B": "all \\(m\\ne0\\)",
    "C": "all \\(m\\ne\\frac12\\)",
    "D": "all \\(m\\ne1\\)",
    "E": "no values of \\(m\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Both equations have the same left side, so subtracting them eliminates \\(y\\). Any common solution must satisfy\n\\[\n0=(1-m)x-1,\n\\]\nor \\((1-m)x=1\\).\n\nIf \\(m=1\\), this would say \\(0=1\\), so there is no solution.\n\nIf \\(m\\ne1\\), choose\n\\[\nx=\\frac{1}{1-m},\\qquad y=mx+3.\n\\]\nThese are real numbers. The two original right sides differ by \\((1-m)x-1=0\\), so this pair satisfies both equations.\n\nTherefore the equations have at least one real solution exactly when \\(\\boxed{m\\ne1}\\), choice D.",
